## Background

`ToolLoopAgent` has `experimental_refineToolInput`, a mapping from tool
name to a function that adjusts the parsed tool input before the tool
runs. `WorkflowAgent` did not have the option. TypeScript rejected it,
and when it was passed anyway it was ignored.

## Summary

- `WorkflowAgent` accepts `experimental_refineToolInput` in the
constructor and per call. The refined input is used for tool execution,
step results, callbacks, and the assistant tool call in the next model
request.
- The functions run in workflow context after the model step returns.
Tools with a runtime validator go through core's `parseToolCall`. Tools
without one (`jsonSchema()` without `validate`) get the refinement
applied directly, and a refinement that throws marks the call invalid,
as in core.
- A tool call that waits for approval is revalidated with the refinement
when the run resumes, so the approved refined input still matches and
the tool runs with it.
- Reference entries for the constructor and `stream()` options. The row
is removed from "Differences from ToolLoopAgent".
- One difference remains and is documented: the `tool-call` chunk that
`stream()` writes to `writable` for a tool without a runtime validator,
and the input of provider-executed tool calls, keep the unrefined value.
- New test file `workflow-agent-refine-tool-input.test.ts`, side by side
with `ToolLoopAgent`.

## End-to-End Verification

Both runs used a Vercel Sandbox (Linux, Node 24.22, pnpm 12.14) with a
fresh clone of `vercel/ai` at `71bef690f9` in `/vercel/sandbox/base`,
after `pnpm install` and `pnpm build`. The scripts live in
`/vercel/sandbox/e2e`, outside the checkout.

- Before: the unmodified tree.
- After: this PR's diff applied with `git apply`, then `pnpm build` in
`packages/workflow`. The scripts were not changed between the runs.

No real model API was called, because the sandbox had no provider
credentials. Part A runs the real `@ai-sdk/openai` provider over HTTP
against a local mock of the Chat Completions API. Part B runs the agent
inside the real Workflow runtime with a mock model.

